<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Lamidate of Ngaoundere</h1><p class="st-meta">1835 CE – 1901 CE (approx.) · capital Ngaoundere</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Fulbe lamidate on the Adamaoua plateau of Cameroon, a sub-emirate of Adamawa and one of the great slave-raiding states of the 19th century.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Ngaoundere was founded around 1835 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardo-njobdi">Ardo Njobdi</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fulbe</a> leader acting under the authority of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/adamawa-emirate">Adamawa Emirate</a>. His forces conquered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mbum-people">Mbum people</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adamawa-plateau">Adamaoua</a> plateau and took over the site of Ngaoundere, which became the seat of a lamido owing allegiance to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yola">Yola</a> and, through it, to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto Caliphate</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The lamidate's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fulbe</a> rulers governed a large <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mbum-people">Mbum</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gbaya-people">Gbaya</a> population, partly incorporated as subjects and partly enslaved. Ngaoundere sent raiding expeditions far to the east and south, into what is now the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-african-republic">Central African Republic</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chad">Chad</a>, and was one of the main sources of slaves in the region. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">Cattle</a> herding on the plateau grasslands was the other pillar of its economy.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>German forces reached Ngaoundere in 1901 and defeated the lamido's army, and it became part of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kamerun">Kamerun</a>, later <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/french-cameroon">French Cameroon</a>. The Lamido of Ngaoundere still reigns from his palace in the city.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="emirate, 1835 CE – 1901 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0